<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<paragraphs xml:id="par-eigen-architecture" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>The Architecture: Networked Mathematical Thinking</title>

  <p>
     To support the first-principles guidelines, :logo: <em>There and Back Again</em> is divided into five functional
     layers. Each has a distinct purpose in the research lifecycle:
  </p>

  <figure xml:id="fig-system-architecture">
    <caption>The Five Functional Layers of Eigenscribe</caption>
    <image xml:id="mermaid-system-architecture">
      <description>A mermaid diagram showing the five functional layers: Frontmatter, Scribing, Practice Problems, Meta, and Backmatter.</description>
      <mermaid>
        graph TD
          FM[Frontmatter]
          SC[Scribing]
          PP[Practice Problems]
          MT[Meta]
          BM[Backmatter]
          FM -- "Rules" --> SC
          SC -- "Application" --> PP
          SC -- "Utilities" --> MT
          PP -- "Reference" --> BM
          MT -- "Reference" --> BM
      </mermaid>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <dl>

    <li>
      <title>Frontmatter</title>
      <p>The "constitution" of the :logo: <em>There and Back Again</em> where conventions, notations, and methodology
      is established.</p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>🪶 Scribing</title>
      <p>
        The "living" layer containing field notes, historical context, and the narrative of discovery.
        This is where <strong>concept bridges</strong> are forged, linking fields like Geometric Algebra and Quantum Mechanics through shared primitives.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>⚽️ Practice Problems</title>
      <p>
        A dedicated library of practice sets, designed to test understanding and reinforce application of theory.
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>🔱Meta</title>
      <p>
        A collection of meta-information and cheatsheets for :logo: <em>Eigenscribe ©</em> :logo: .
      </p>
    </li>

    <li>
      <title>Backmatter</title>
      <p>
        Foundational appendices, glossary and index terms, and a bibliography.
      </p>
    </li>

  </dl>

</paragraphs>
